// Practice quiz: Promises
// First draft: unreviewed. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: html-css-js/dom-tooling-and-async/promises

::o1 settled once::A promise was fulfilled. Later, code calls its reject function. What is its state? {
=Still fulfilled #Correct. A promise settles only once, and later calls are ignored.
~Rejected #Not quite. Settled state never changes.
~Pending again #Not quite. A promise never returns to pending.
~Both fulfilled and rejected #Not quite. It has exactly one outcome.
}

::o1 starting state::What state is a promise in while its work has not finished? {
=Pending #Correct. It stays pending until it settles.
~Fulfilled with undefined #Not quite. Fulfilment happens only when resolved.
~Rejected #Not quite. Rejection happens only on failure.
~Blocked #Not quite. That is not a promise state, and nothing blocks.
}

::o2 return in then::A then callback has a block body that fetches a value but does not return it. What does the next then receive? {
=undefined #Correct. The chain carries what the callback returns, and nothing was returned.
~The fetched value #Not quite. That needs a return.
~The previous value again #Not quite. The previous value is replaced by the callback result.
~An error #Not quite. Returning nothing is not an error.
}

::o2 thrown in then::Code in a then callback throws. Where does control go? {
=To the next catch in the chain, because a throw becomes a rejection #Correct. Thrown errors reject the promise returned by then.
~To the surrounding try and catch block around the chain #Not quite. That block finished long before the callback ran.
~Nowhere\: the error is silently discarded #Not quite. It rejects, and is reported if unhandled.
~To the next then #Not quite. Then callbacks for fulfilment are skipped.
}

::o3 result order::Promise.all receives a 300 ms task then a 100 ms task. Both succeed. What order is the result array? {
=The 300 ms result first, matching input order #Correct. Results follow input order, not finish order.
~The 100 ms result first #Not quite. Completion order does not decide the array.
~Random #Not quite. The order is guaranteed.
~Only the fastest result #Not quite. That describes Promise.race.
}
